{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1554590-how-do-outcasts-even-work","id":1554590,"title":"How do outcasts even work?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Curseborne"],"posts":[{"date":"2025-10-23T01:41:12-07:00","id":1554590,"title":"How do outcasts even work?","content":"Mainly i am confused about the way their slip mask movement option works.<br /> Is it something you choose at chargen? Do you choose them when you slip off the mask?<br /> And if you choose it at chargen, how do you choose how this movement option works? It's not like we have a movement type table like the movement options megaedge in aberrant or anything.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27340-anyoneatall","name":"Anyoneatall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-25T07:45:40-07:00","id":1554662,"title":"","content":"You choose at character creation, when you decide what the Outcast's Liminal Form looks like. It does not affect how much distance you can cover in a single movement, only what kinds of obstacles you circumvent. A cloud-riding jinn, for instance, can move vertically with the same ease as across the ground and even hover, allowing them to just ignore any hazards located on the ground, but still moves at the same speed as anyone else. The main function of the trait is that Outcasts don't really care about little inconveniences like &quot;difficult terrain.&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-29T01:13:57-07:00","id":1554760,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>r0gue_rob0t</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1554590-how-do-outcasts-even-work?p=1554662#post1554662\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">You choose at character creation, when you decide what the Outcast's Liminal Form looks like. It does not affect how much distance you can cover in a single movement, only what kinds of obstacles you circumvent. A cloud-riding jinn, for instance, can move vertically with the same ease as across the ground and even hover, allowing them to just ignore any hazards located on the ground, but still moves at the same speed as anyone else. The main function of the trait is that Outcasts don't really care about little inconveniences like &quot;difficult terrain.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Wdym? Like, could you expand on this?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27340-anyoneatall","name":"Anyoneatall","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":1,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2022"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-10-29T19:57:20-07:00","id":1554811,"title":"","content":"Whatever the movement option chosen for the character is, you still only move one &quot;range band&quot; (close -&gt; short, etc.) per reflexive move action, with the usual alternative move actions that take a simple/mixed action, etc. Slipping Off the Mask doesn't change the distance your movement covers, it only grants you access to normal movement in situations where normal movement would normally not be available: flight and hovering let you use normal movement to go up into the air, spider limbs let you use normal movement to traverse walls and ceilings, fins and the like remove any Athletics rolls in the water that wouldn't be part of normal movement on the ground, etc.<br /> <br /> The specifics of your chosen method serve to determine when Athletics rolls and Complications might come up - an Outcast with spider legs won't suffer Complications on Athletics rolls for air turbulence like a winged Outcast would, but may for especially low-friction surfaces, for example. Similarly, the jumping Outcast can get one range band into the air, but has to come back down next turn, while being unaffected by the Complications I described for both of the others. This is why flight isn't treated as if it's &quot;better&quot; than other options - flight is notably less useful in a storm without Spells to offset related area effects, and even with Spells, it doesn't do much indoors.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2025-11-01T14:20:08-07:00","id":1554923,"title":"","content":"Thinking on it, I do have my own questions about Outcasts, though. Namely: healing and, separately, loopholes (which seem sadly under-explored in general).<br /> <br /> For healing: The Dead get to roll Esoterica in resonant area effects without need of supplies to heal their spirit-selves, Hungry and Primals have built-in (activated) regeneration, and Sorcerers have a handful of healing Spells (with a better version of life steal than the Hungry get, oddly enough, while the other Spell is more useful for removing status effects than injuries). Outcasts get... nothing. Unless they take Summon Imp (shared from Sorcerers - and reliant on the fickleness of imps), they're entirely limited to completely mundane Medicine rolls, with only Assistive Construct (and Executive Function) to make the mundane roll easier. Even the preview content for the Player's Guide doesn't add anything, despite the obvious opportunity in the Ambrosia Spell.<br /> <br /> It seems odd to me that the exact Lineage most oriented toward the miraculous would be <i>the least capable</i> of performing one of the most obvious miracles anyone would immediately think of: (faith) healing. Did I miss something somewhere?<br /> <br /> Beyond that, with loopholes (this one also affects Primals): do Outcasts who enter a loophole while their liminal form is exposed retain said form, if not its more magical traits? I feel like this is something that would have a <i>huge</i> impact on how Outcasts perceive loopholes - getting forcibly shoved back into a human-shaped box might be convenient when struggling with Damnation, but might feel <i>oppressive</i> - a harsh reminder that this world <i>does not</i> welcome them - at other times.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3388-r0gue_rob0t","name":"r0gue_rob0t","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"29"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-03-21T01:33:12-07:00","id":1558957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>r0gue_rob0t</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/curseborne/1554590-how-do-outcasts-even-work?p=1554923#post1554923\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Thinking on it, I do have my own questions about Outcasts, though. Namely: healing and, separately, loopholes (which seem sadly under-explored in general).<br /> <br /> For healing: The Dead get to roll Esoterica in resonant area effects without need of supplies to heal their spirit-selves, Hungry and Primals have built-in (activated) regeneration, and Sorcerers have a handful of healing Spells (with a better version of life steal than the Hungry get, oddly enough, while the other Spell is more useful for removing status effects than injuries). Outcasts get... nothing. Unless they take Summon Imp (shared from Sorcerers - and reliant on the fickleness of imps), they're entirely limited to completely mundane Medicine rolls, with only Assistive Construct (and Executive Function) to make the mundane roll easier. Even the preview content for the Player's Guide doesn't add anything, despite the obvious opportunity in the Ambrosia Spell.<br /> <br /> It seems odd to me that the exact Lineage most oriented toward the miraculous would be <i>the least capable</i> of performing one of the most obvious miracles anyone would immediately think of: (faith) healing. Did I miss something somewhere?<br /> <br /> Beyond that, with loopholes (this one also affects Primals): do Outcasts who enter a loophole while their liminal form is exposed retain said form, if not its more magical traits? I feel like this is something that would have a <i>huge</i> impact on how Outcasts perceive loopholes - getting forcibly shoved back into a human-shaped box might be convenient when struggling with Damnation, but might feel <i>oppressive</i> - a harsh reminder that this world <i>does not</i> welcome them - at other times.</div> </div> </div> </div>The lack of Outcast healing abilities struck me as particularly egregious as well. I guess the League of the Hidden Crossroads gets a healing spell, which is weird when the Keepers of the Broken Vine are the Family singled out for their healing abilities, but for the Linneage as a whole, it's really strange they get nothing to get back on their feet faster after a fight, or as you said can't do what would be the most obvious miracle one would associate with gods, angels, and co.<br /> <br /> Edit: Could an Outcast's Assistive Contruct have the Potion Junkie Dread Power and use the Regrowth Spell? Could the Construct use the Merge Dread Power to fuse with a character and have them benefit from its Regeneration Quality?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>When will you rage?","edited":{"userid":1405,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","user":"Anteros","date":".  New ideas for potential Outcast healing 03-28-2026, 03:10 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1405-anteros","name":"Anteros","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1405_4.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"661"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}